WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.083
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:08.851
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:16.134
Unit twenty-three: Space Exploration. Today you will predict completed future actions with the future perfect.

4
00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:19.074
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.173
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.017 --> 00:00:25.145
Space.

7
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.788
Space means the area beyond Earth's air.

8
00:00:29.383 --> 00:00:31.453
Space is huge and mysterious.

9
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.308
Your turn. Say: Space.

10
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:38.365
Space.

11
00:00:39.117 --> 00:00:40.106
Astronaut.

12
00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:44.266
Astronaut means a person who travels into space.

13
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.931
The astronaut floated in the station.

14
00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:49.786
Your turn. Say: Astronaut.

15
00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:53.634
Astronaut.

16
00:00:54.383 --> 00:00:55.268
Satellite.

17
00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:59.212
Satellite means an object that goes around a planet.

18
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:01.956
Satellites help us use GPS.

19
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.040
Your turn. Say: Satellite.

20
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.052
Satellite.

21
00:01:09.817 --> 00:01:10.562
Rocket.

22
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.594
Rocket means a vehicle that flies into space.

23
00:01:15.183 --> 00:01:16.765
The rocket launched at dawn.

24
00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.700
Your turn. Say: Rocket.

25
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:23.347
Rocket.

26
00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:25.003
Mission.

27
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:28.527
Mission means an important journey with a goal.

28
00:01:29.117 --> 00:01:31.058
The Mars mission will take years.

29
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:34.384
Your turn. Say: Mission.

30
00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:38.153
Mission.

31
00:01:38.917 --> 00:01:39.720
Planet.

32
00:01:40.717 --> 00:01:43.401
Planet means a large world that goes around a star.

33
00:01:44.017 --> 00:01:45.680
Mars is the red planet.

34
00:01:46.283 --> 00:01:48.703
Your turn. Say: Planet.

35
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:52.347
Planet.

36
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:54.096
Research.

37
00:01:55.083 --> 00:01:58.197
Research means careful study to discover facts.

38
00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:01.270
Space research helps medicine too.

39
00:02:01.883 --> 00:02:04.988
Your turn. Say: Research.

40
00:02:07.833 --> 00:02:08.707
Research.

41
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.637
Cooperation.

42
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.777
Cooperation means working together.

43
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:17.079
The space station is built on cooperation.

44
00:02:17.683 --> 00:02:20.777
Your turn. Say: Cooperation.

45
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:24.727
Cooperation.

46
00:02:25.533 --> 00:02:29.080
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:29.917 --> 00:02:33.077
Visal, what will happen in space by 2040?

48
00:02:34.183 --> 00:02:38.946
By 2040, astronauts will have visited Mars.

49
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.550
Do you really think so?

50
00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:47.740
Yes. By then, scientists will have built bases on the Moon.

51
00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.269
Is it worth the cost?

52
00:02:51.383 --> 00:02:57.089
I think so. Space research will have helped medicine and technology too.

53
00:02:58.233 --> 00:02:59.943
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:00.783 --> 00:03:03.154
Grammar focus: Future perfect.

55
00:03:03.454 --> 00:03:04.583
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:08.583
By 2040, astronauts will have visited Mars.

57
00:03:09.483 --> 00:03:12.412
By then, scientists will have built bases on the Moon.

58
00:03:13.317 --> 00:03:17.534
By 2050, we will have sent many more satellites.

59
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:23.344
Use 'will have' + past participle for things that will be finished before a future time.

60
00:03:24.083 --> 00:03:29.383
Be careful! Don't say: By 2040, astronauts will have visit Mars.

61
00:03:31.333 --> 00:03:35.481
Say: By 2040, astronauts will have visited Mars.

62
00:03:35.781 --> 00:03:38.303
After 'will have', use the past participle.

63
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.601
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.900
Reaching for the Stars.

65
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.744
Space exploration is one of humanity's greatest adventures.

66
00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:52.103
Rockets carry astronauts and satellites into space.

67
00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.748
By 2040, astronauts will have visited Mars.

68
00:03:57.733 --> 00:04:00.836
Space research has already improved life on Earth.

69
00:04:01.833 --> 00:04:05.180
International cooperation makes every mission possible.

70
00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:10.025
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:14.334
By 2040, astronauts will have blank Mars.

72
00:04:14.634 --> 00:04:18.230
A, visited. B, visit. C, visiting.

73
00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:23.604
The answer is A: visited.

74
00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.760
A person who travels into space is an blank.

75
00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:31.957
A, astronaut. B, satellite. C, rocket.

76
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.267
The answer is A: astronaut.

77
00:04:38.417 --> 00:04:41.113
An object that goes around a planet is a blank.

78
00:04:41.413 --> 00:04:45.507
A, satellite. B, mission. C, star.

79
00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:51.182
The answer is A: satellite.

80
00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:55.495
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

81
00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:59.606
Is space exploration worth the cost? Why?

82
00:05:08.417 --> 00:05:10.021
Now listen to Visal.

83
00:05:10.321 --> 00:05:19.975
I think it is worth the cost. By 2050, space research will have given us new medicines and better weather forecasts, which help everyone.

84
00:05:20.275 --> 00:05:21.937
Excellent answer, Visal!

85
00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:26.791
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future perfect.

86
00:05:27.091 --> 00:05:30.846
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

87
00:05:31.146 --> 00:05:33.973
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

88
00:05:34.273 --> 00:05:36.855
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

